WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Neutrinos reveal how supernovas explode.
And they also warn us when one is about

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:12.440
to detonate.
>> So, neutrinos can even be these ghostly

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:16.520
signposts for something very violent
that's happened in the universe, right?

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:21.280
We detect a sudden burst of neutrinos,
it could be that a star has gone

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.280
supernova somewhere.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:30.800
>> Neutrino bursts are cosmic watchdogs,
alerting us to danger.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:35.080
>> Neutrinos are definitely a sign that
something troubling is happening.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:49.480
>> Our solar system is a celestial cold
case, and it's hiding the traces of its

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.600
violent past.
>> What you have now

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:58.600
is a crime scene that has dried up, and
you're trying to find little clues as to

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:04.320
what [music] happened 4 and 1/2 billion
years ago. It's a really, really

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.320
difficult problem [music] to solve.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:12.080
>> Solid evidence can be hard to find, but
sometimes we get lucky.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:15.040
>> We don't have a time machine, so it's
hard to go back in time 4 and 1/2

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.000
billion years and look at the solar
system and see what it was doing back

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:24.640
then. However, sometimes nature
provides, and if you don't have a time

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:29.360
machine, sometimes a time capsule will
do just as well. And in fact, we have

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.880
time capsules of the early solar system,
and we call them meteorites.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:41.400
>> Most meteorites are chunks of asteroids
that fall to Earth. Depending on their

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:45.800
origin, they come in different shapes
and sizes.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:52.080
>> Asteroids really are like space fossils
because they were formed 4 and 1/2

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:55.400
billion years ago, but they've basically
remained dead.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.560
>> They are the leftovers, the remnants of
planet formation. They're the last

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:02.280
little bits that haven't become planets
yet.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.039
>> To understand why meteorites are such
useful clues,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:13.320
we first need to know how planets form.
It's a process called accretion.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:18.959
The cloud of hot gas swirling around the
Sun condenses and clumps into larger and

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:27.200
larger bodies. We find traces of this
process inside meteorites in tiny

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:31.000
mineral beads called chondrules.
>> Chondrules are literally the seeds of

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.600
all of the structure in our solar
system.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:40.400
>> Most chondrules condense out of the
cloud of hot gas around the Sun as the

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:45.800
solar system forms.
>> Chondrules have been described

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:52.560
poetically as droplets of fiery rain
that have solidified. They are little

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:57.920
globules of silicate melt that were
produced in the very earliest history of

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:02.320
our solar system. These globules of melt
solidified to form these little spheres

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:08.360
that really tells us about the process
of agglomeration of smaller objects to

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.360
form larger bodies.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:15.959
>> But some chondrules tell us not only
about a planet's birth, but also its

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:15.959
death.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:31.120
>> A meteorite called Gujba contains two
very different kinds of globules.

00:03:30.912 --> 00:03:37.360
>> [music]
>> So, Gujba is a type of meteorite that's

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:43.200
made up of um
little spherules of silicate material as

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:47.959
well as spherules of iron nickel metal.
And it's very unusual in that regard.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:54.200
These metal spherules that we find in
Gujba are formed, we think, around 5 or

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.680
6 million years after the solar system
forms.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:04.800
>> At that point, there was not enough hot
gas lingering in the disk to form the

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:09.800
chondrules we see in Gujba.
So, how did these [music] globules form?

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:15.520
>> The only way to really produce these
globules is another process, and we

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.640
think in this case it was some kind of
process like collisions.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:28.240
>> Collisions so violent they vaporized the
silicates and metals.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:32.640
Solid turns to gas, and then gas to
liquid.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:38.400
But, what planetary body contains enough
metal to be able to produce the droplets

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:43.960
we see in Gujba?
Only something big enough to have an

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.960
iron core.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:49.440
>> When an object grows large enough, its
gravity becomes strong enough that it

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.040
differentiates. And what we mean by that
is

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.280
heavy stuff is pulled down and sinks
into the center, and lighter stuff

00:04:53.520 --> 00:05:01.240
floats to the top. So, you have a
differentiation of material.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:05.840
>> Earth is a classic example of a body
that is differentiated.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:11.200
Heavy metals like iron and nickel sink
to the core, while lighter rocky

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:16.680
silicates remain in the crust.
The crust and the core are separated by

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:22.800
a molten silicate layer known as the
mantle.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:28.480
>> Gujba is a perfect example of the fact
that you had a large planetary bodies

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.919
that were differentiated into irons and
silicates, and they were colliding at

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:38.240
velocities great enough to scatter their
pieces out into the nebula again.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:42.919
>> That is just amazing to me that this
really, really violent

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.880
process in history that that's captured
in these tiny little fragments.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:55.880
>> Gujba reveals the differentiated planets
were commonplace as early as 5 million

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.400
years after the formation of the solar
system.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:04.240
There were so many of these planets,
they often smash together.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:13.400
>> If you go down this path of uh planet
formation by giant impacts, you end up

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:18.080
with the last survivors being a bunch of
freaks.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:21.280
>> Freaks built from the dead embers of
past generations.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:28.160
And this violent scenario raises the
question,

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:31.880
just how many planets did it take to
build the inner solar system?

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:39.840
Simulations say at least 30,
but just four survived.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:50.280
The universe is full of galaxies. From a
distance, they look calm and peaceful.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:55.360
Look a little closer, and you'll see
evidence of extreme violence and

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.360
destruction.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:04.480
Scars extending from their centers out
tens of thousands of light-years.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:11.360
What could have caused such [music]
devastation?

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:16.320
This is galaxy cluster Hydra A.
Here are the scars.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:19.520
But viewing it in multiple wavelengths
reveals the culprit.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:27.720
Two colossal [music] jets of energy
blasting out from the galactic core,

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:32.600
shooting out from the heart of a quasar.
